;;; A top-level fact splits a name that starts with `relation` — M1e2 **W22**,
;;; register row `W22/kw-relation-fact` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), banked
;;; by S1e2.2.22 (2026-11-05) from the inventory of 2026-11-05's F1, on the
;;; user's ruling of that day (Q-M1e2.16).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ──────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two people, `alice` and `bob`, a relation `relationship` between two
;;; `Node`s, and one fact, `(relationship alice bob)`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A keyword is a whole word (`00_ebnf.md` § 1, fact 1), so `relationship` is
;;; a name, and the fact is a fact. Root holds `(relationship alice bob)`; the
;;; blind enumerator proposes `(relationship bob alice)`, and R is two states:
;;; the answer of `kw-relation-fact-twin.ein`.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and why it does not any more ────────────
;;;
;;; Until S1e2.2.22 the parser read a reserved word as a prefix of a name, and
;;; this program answered wrong. The parser read `(relationship alice bob)` as
;;; `(relation ship alice bob)`, a declaration of a relation `ship` whose
;;; signature is `alice bob`, so the program stated no `relationship` fact.
;;; `alice` and `bob` were types, H was empty and R was {∅}. (`ein solve FILE`
;;; at `35186bc0`, release and debug; every mode and `ein oracle` the same.)
;;; Since then a keyword is a whole word, at the boundary `00_ebnf.md` § 1
;;; states (the user's ruling of 2026-11-05 on Q-M1e2.17), and the program
;;; answers its twin's R.

(relation person Node :why "{?1} is a person") (relation relationship Node Node :why "{?1} has a relationship with {?2}")
(person alice) (person bob)
(relationship alice bob)
